  <revision rev="8" vrev="5">
    <srcmd5>481660ca4981fe1922d6818fb2e4d7e6</srcmd5>
    <version>0.2.0</version>
    <time>1788270427</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>Add an explicit Requires: soapy-sdr to the module subpackage.

The module links libSoapySDR, so it has always had an automatic soname
dependency (libSoapySDR.so.0.8-3), and the loader itself - the
SoapySDR_loadModules symbol - lives in that library. So the library was
never missing. What the module never pulled in is the soapy-sdr package,
whose only content is /usr/bin/SoapySDRUtil. This is therefore a usability
dependency, not a functional one: a module with no client on the system is
useless, which is the point raised in review.

It is not a match of existing practice: no soapy-* plugin package in Factory
today carries this Requires. Prompted by the review comment on sr#1374694
(soapy-plutosdr, since superseded by sr#1374737 and sr#1374739 - the comment
is on the original, dead request). The rest of the SoapySDR plugin family is
getting the same treatment.

Also in this request: use %cmake_build instead of a bare make call, plus a
small spec cleanup (pkg-config -&gt; pkgconfig, copyright header).

No version change. 0.2.0 is still the newest upstream tag; master carries 5
unreleased commits (a debug-log format fix and CMake modernisation), and
NixOS, FreeBSD and Void all ship 0.2.0, so the tag stands.</comment>
    <requestid>1374752</requestid>
  </revision>
